# **8. Scenario 5 — Exceptional (The Ceiling)**

*Not a plan. Not an expectation. Included to show the theoretical upside if word-of-mouth compounds virally and org reach extends significantly beyond initial targets. Treat as context, not forecast.*

## **Assumptions**

| Orgs sharing code | 50+ organisations by end of Year 1. National reach across all three categories. |
| --- | --- |
| Taster code conversion | 10% redeem. 35% convert to paid. |
| Beta testers | 200+ people. 25% convert. |
| General free users | 2,500+ by end of Year 1. |
| Free to paid | 8% |
| Monthly churn | 2% — exceptional retention. |
| Word of mouth | Viral coefficient >1 — each user brings in more than one new user. |

## **Summary projections only**

| **Year** | **Active paid users** | **Gross revenue** | **Net revenue** | **Position** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Year 1 | 350+ | ~£220,000 | ~£211,000 | Immediate income replacement possibility. |
| Year 2 | 1,100+ | ~£780,000 | ~£748,000 | Established multi-person business. |
| Year 3 | 2,500+ | ~£1,800,000 | ~£1,727,000 | Series A territory. Institutional licensing live. |
| Year 4 | 4,500+ | ~£3,200,000 | ~£3,071,000 | Category-defining product. |

*Exceptional verdict: included for completeness and investor conversations only. Do not plan against this scenario. Execute against Optimistic Likely. Be delighted if Exceptional arrives.*

# **9. Five-Scenario Summary — Year 4**

| **Scenario** | **Break-even** | **Y1 net** | **Y2 net** | **Y3 net** | **Y4 net** | **Y4 paid users** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **1. Floor** | Q3–Q4 Y1 | £3,137 | £8,900 | ~£17,200 | ~£26,800 | ~110 |
| **2. Pessimistic Likely** | Q2 Y1 | £12,375 | £44,900 | ~£69,000 | ~£113,000 | ~360 |
| **3. Optimistic Likely** | Q1 Y1 | £42,023 | £185,700 | ~£297,000 | ~£470,000 | ~850 |
| **4. Strong Traction** | Q1 Y1 | £101,492 | £397,900 | ~£585,000 | ~£930,000 | ~1,500 |
| **5. Exceptional** | Month 1 | ~£211,000 | ~£748,000 | ~£1,727,000 | ~£3,071,000 | ~4,500 |

*The most important variable across all five scenarios remains monthly churn. A 1% improvement in churn has more long-term impact than doubling new user acquisition. This is why the philosophy is the product — and why retention is the business model.*

*Plan against Scenario 2 (Pessimistic Likely). Execute as if Scenario 3 (Optimistic Likely) is achievable. Treat Scenario 4 as the reward for sustained excellent execution. Scenario 5 is not a plan.*

